description:
pkix-lint/src/oscal/profile.rs:203-206 — resolve_profile signature:

  pub fn resolve_profile(
      profile: &Value,
      sources: &HashMap<String, Value>,
  ) -> Result<ResolvedProfile, ParseError>

The sources parameter is &HashMap<String, Value>. This fixes:
- the storage shape (HashMap, not BTreeMap, IndexMap, or any other Map),
- the key shape (String, not Cow<'static, str> or &str),
- the value shape (serde_json::Value — already separately flagged for the broader leakage concern).

CALLER IMPACT: A caller whose source profiles live in BTreeMap (deterministic iteration), IndexMap (insertion-ordered), or a file-fetched lazy cache must allocate a fresh HashMap snapshot to call this function. The HashMap allocation is forced even for one-shot one-source use.

The function does not actually need HashMap semantics — it only does .get(href) lookups (oscal/profile.rs:228, search for `sources.get`). Any keyed lookup works.

REMEDIATION: Accept a callback or trait object instead of a concrete map:

  pub fn resolve_profile(
      profile: &Value,
      sources: impl Fn(&str) -> Option<&Value>,
  ) -> Result<ResolvedProfile, ParseError>

Or with a small trait for ergonomics:
  pub trait OscalProfileSource {
      fn get(&self, href: &str) -> Option<&Value>;
  }
  impl OscalProfileSource for HashMap<String, Value> { ... }
  impl OscalProfileSource for BTreeMap<String, Value> { ... }
  // plus blanket impl for Fn

Callers with a HashMap still pass it directly. Callers with anything else aren't forced to allocate a HashMap.

This finding is orthogonal to (but stacked with) the serde_json::Value leakage on the same function — fix both together for a clean signature, or fix this one independently if the serde_json migration is deferred.

Pre-1.0 free.
